          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %em higher regulator
            %em regulator
            for short, is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            to a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            theory. (It makes sense to think that it “regulates”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regulator+of+a+number+field"} regulator of a number field
            which is a measure for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+integers"} ring of integers
            of the number field, in view of the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant"} determinant
            function provides a canonical homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi GL
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1 \to GL_1
            from the first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
          %p
            Regulators are used in the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-functions"} L-functions
            for instance in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson+conjectures"} Beilinson conjectures
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch-Kato+conjecture"} Bloch-Kato conjecture
          %p The simplest example of such regulators are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+class+number+formula"} analytic class number formula
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+zeta-function"} Dedekind zeta-function
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %li
              %p the Dirichlet regulator map, discussed below.
          %p
            These regulators may be understood as essentially being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+characters"} Chern characters
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#DeningerScholl"}> Deninger Scholl (2.6)
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Tamme12"} Tamme 12
            = succeed "-refinements" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+algebraic+K-theory"} differential algebraic K-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeTamme12"}> Bunke-Tamme 12
            ).
          %blockquote
            %p
              we usually call transformations from K-theory to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+homology"} cyclic homology
              Chern characters, and transformations from K-theory to differential forms regulators. There is one exception, namely the usual Chern character from topological K-theory to cohomology with complex coefficients calculated by the de Rham cohomology. (
              %a{:href => "#Bunke14"}> Bunke 14, remark 2.20
              )
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#BorelRegulator Dirichlet and Borel regulator
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K R
            denoting its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %mi H
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^n H \mathbb{R}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} suspension
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi K
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi p
                  %mi H
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  r_{\sigma,p} \;\colon\; K R \longrightarrow \Sigma^p H \mathbb{R}
          %p
            or equivalently is the induced
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+operations"} cohomology operations
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BunkeTamme12"} Bunke-Tamme 12, 1.2
          %p
            If here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+integers"} ring of integers
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+field"} number field
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi R
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \colon R \hookrightarrow \mathbb{C}
            is a choice of embedding into the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %em Borel regulator
            (
            %a{:href => "#Borel74"}> Borel 74
            ) is of this form, for odd
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+operation"} cohomology operation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  r_{\sigma,p} \;\colon\; K_1(R) \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            is the
            %em Dirichlet regulator
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi log
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  u \mapsto log {\vert \sigma(u) \vert}
                  \\,.
          %p
            A description of this in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+algebraic+K-theory"} differential algebraic K-theory
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeTamme12"}> Bunke-Tamme 12, 1.2
            ):
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K R^0(X)
            is represented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+generated+module"} finitely generated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+module"} projective
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+bundle"} module bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \to X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi cycl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi K
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  cycl(V) \in K R^0(X)
          %p for this class.
          %p
            Under the chosen embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            we have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexification"} complexification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi σ
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \otimes_\sigma \mathbb{C}
            of this
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+bundle"} module bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+bundle"} complex vector bundle
            with (because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is geometrically
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{V_\sigma}
          %p
            The choice of hermitean structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction+of+the+structure+group"} reduction of the structure group
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} unitary group
            induces an adjoint connection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi V
                          %mi σ
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{V_\sigma}^\ast
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi CS
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∇
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mi σ
                    %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CS_p(\nabla_{V_\sigma}^\ast, \nabla_{V_\sigma})
          %p
            for the relative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+form"} Chern-Simons form
            between these two connections, hence for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
            of the relative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            in degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+1
          %p
            This is a closed differential form, (the Kamber-Tondeur form, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BismutLott95"} Bismut-Lott 95
          %p
            This differential form represents, via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"} de Rham theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi cycl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi CS
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
